The signature given in spec: `fn splice(tree: SumTree<u8>, range: std::ops::Range<usize>, new_items: Vec<u8>) -> SumTree<u8>`. Takes ownership of tree — fine since test_random does `tree = { ... }` consuming tree via cursor borrow inside block then reassign. With ownership it's simpler.

```rust
fn splice(tree: SumTree<u8>, range: std::ops::Range<usize>, new_items: Vec<u8>) -> SumTree<u8> {
    let tree_end = tree.extent::<Count>(()).0;
    let mut cursor = tree.cursor::<Count>(());
    let mut new_tree = cursor.slice(&Count(range.start), Bias::Right);
    new_tree.extend(new_items, ());
    cursor.seek(&Count(range.end), Bias::Right);
    new_tree.append(cursor.slice(&Count(tree_end), Bias::Right), ());
    new_tree
}
```

Check: `cursor.slice(&Count(range.start), ...)` — cursor borrows tree immutably; new_tree built; then `cursor.seek(...)`; then second slice; then append; return. tree is borrowed by cursor until end of function — fine since we return new_tree only. extend on new_tree with Vec<u8> — extend takes IntoIterator<Item=T>; Vec<u8> yields u8. Good.

extent::<Count>(()).0 — usize. Count(range.end) — Count(usize). Good.

